{
 "id": "invisible-gorilla",
 "channel": "philosophy",
 "style": "clay",
 "title": "THE INVISIBLE GORILLA",
 "angle": "How can you miss a gorilla walking right in front of you?",
 "answer": "Because attention, not the eyes, decides what we see: about half of viewers counting passes missed the gorilla, 83% of expert radiologists missed one in a lung scan while looking at it, a police officer was convicted after saying he missed a beating that scientists later showed people really do miss, and knowing about the trick doesn't protect you from the next one.",
 "logline": "Half the viewers missed a gorilla, so how? Counting passes takes your attention, so the gorilla never registers, but experts must be different, but 83% of radiologists missed a gorilla in a lung scan, but the stakes became real when a Boston officer was convicted for not seeing a beating, so scientists restaged it and most runners at night missed a fight too, but knowing the trick doesn't help, because viewers who expected the gorilla missed other changes, so you see what you look for.",
 "hook_text": "HALF OF THEM MISSED IT",
 "hooks": [
  "83 percent of radiologists missed a gorilla in a lung scan.",
  "A police officer was convicted for not seeing something you might miss too."
 ],
 "world": {
  "scenery": "default"
 },
 "script": [
  {
   "text": "Half the people in this experiment didn't see a gorilla.",
   "beat": "hook"
  },
  {
   "text": "It walked through the middle of the scene and thumped its chest.",
   "beat": "hook",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "So how can anyone miss something that obvious?",
   "beat": "question",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "In 1999, two Harvard psychologists asked viewers to count basketball passes.",
   "say": "In nineteen ninety-nine, two Harvard psychologists asked viewers to count basketball passes.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "answer"
  },
  {
   "text": "Midway, a woman in a gorilla suit strolled through for about nine seconds.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "About half never noticed: their eyes saw it, but their attention was on the ball.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "But surely experts trained to spot the unusual do better?",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "In 2013, radiologists searched lung scans for tiny cancer nodules.",
   "say": "In twenty thirteen, radiologists searched lung scans for tiny cancer nodules.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "answer"
  },
  {
   "text": "A gorilla 48 times bigger than a nodule was hidden in the last scan.",
   "say": "A gorilla forty-eight times bigger than a nodule was hidden in the last scan.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "83 percent missed it, and eye-tracking showed many looked straight at it.",
   "say": "Eighty-three percent missed it, and eye-tracking showed many looked straight at it.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "But the stakes turned real in a courtroom.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "A Boston police officer, chasing a suspect, ran right past a brutal beating, and said he saw nothing.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "He was convicted of lying; years later, one of the gorilla psychologists restaged the chase.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "At night, most runners missed a staged fight beside the path.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "But knowing the trick doesn't protect you.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "Viewers who expected the gorilla spotted it, and missed a curtain changing colour.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "because"
  },
  {
   "text": "So you don't see what's there; you see what you're looking for.",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "What walked right past you today?",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "so"
  }
 ],
